			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475697">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The act was maddening; always chewing, never swallowing, but it kept him from a deeper madness.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is awkward I think.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Joshua packed up his camp and made his way down the silent highway; a nettle-grove of empty cars and twisted rebar.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The semicolon doesn't work here; semicolons are used to join complete clauses.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He was using the highway for it's intended purpose; a quick route from here to there.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Same for this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He'd been staring at the window, and had forgotten his surroundings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would recommend removing explaining the exactness of Joshua's thoughts and internal state. You speak more powerfully with action than in explanation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They were coming for him now.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This similar. You don't need to explain to me the meaning of events when they are easily gleaned by their happenings.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Birds would have taken wing, flying up from the nearby trees… but of course, neither had existed in a long, long time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this would've been better stated to simply state the absence of response.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They never had much neck on them, just a thin chicken bone propping up the head.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Pick one of the two here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Useless.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At this point I'd like for you to take an evaluation of what tools you are using to describe. What are your senses you're using?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He chewed so hard his gums began to bleed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, you can do a lot more here. There's taste of blood. There's smell of fat. There's a dirty rag being chewed on for hours and hours. It coats the throat. It chafes the cheeks. Push.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>dark red liquid</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You have an opportunity here to write a stronger adjective in the place of two weak ones.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>, just performing your own blood test</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove 'just'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She didn’t have any choice. There was no one else left.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unecessary again. At the point where someone is pulling marrow out of themselves it's clear they're out of options.</p>
<p>Again, in this scene, not enough senses for a space so greusome. Imagine the smell of the rot. Imagine how the disease lingers in the air like a mucousy film.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He wore an unmistakable expression. Otto chose his next words much more carefully.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think this is necessary either.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She became a certified accountant, and her co-workers quickly realized that, for whatever reason, Mona knew how to handle stress like a hardened soldier. She was running the company in less than a decade.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would suggest a particualrly stressful subfield of accounting, because a layman would not understand why stress management is useful here.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Another issue, architecturally, is that there isn't really change or progress in the first scene also. In general, I think each individual scene wasn't strong enough to carry the overall piece for me. I referenced in the collapsible a variety of strategies that could be used to make the prose tighter.</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>Living the dream, or dreaming the life?</p>

				</div>
					
					
