			<div class="content" id="post-content-4486028">
									

<p>I have to say, I'm just incredibly impressed by this entry. The setup for all the different iterations is clever (and captures the whole &quot;there-is-no-canon&quot; spirit in the best way), and the execution of the various end-of-the-world tales is just top-notch throughout. The prose is compelling, and each entry does a great job of capturing that feeling of end-of-the-world desperation in a short space (with some especially effective uses of understatement, like the 049 crosslink and the whole Death/2935 section).</p>
<p>I'm sure there'll be some of the traditional complaints about how &quot;this reads more like a tale than a SCP file,&quot; but the way this article plays around with the standard format feels anything but arbitrary. Rather, it's a means to the end of telling an effective story, one that wouldn't have quite so memorable of an impact in a more standard format. The ending flash-forward caught me by surprise, but it definitely highlights the themes you referenced in your author post. All in all, an easy +1 from me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
